From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:05:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a member that refused this conversation as too long
 is asked last, not first
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

What changed: an overflow (400, "prompt is too long") is the request's
length, not the account's, so it rests nobody — and left no trace: the
next request of the same conversation tried the member again, failed the
same way, and fell back again, for every request (#3453: a rule sent
every turn to a Claude subscription bridge that answered "Prompt is too
long" and DeepSeek answered; two consecutive requests of one turn each
paid the refused try first). Now the conversation remembers, per
provider's model, how large the request was when it was refused, and the
member is put behind the others — behind, not out: it is still asked
when every other member has failed, a rule that names it is still
heard (ruleOver has run), and what it answers makes magpie forget the
refusal. A request smaller than what was refused — the agent has
compacted the conversation — finds the member where the rule put it.
Another conversation is unaffected, and a byte cap (413) is not
remembered: it is not the conversation too long for the model.

Run: GOCACHE=... GOMODCACHE=... go test -tags nogui -run
'TestOverflowRefusedOnceIsAskedLast' ./internal/gateway/ (red on
4683a038, green here); go test -tags nogui ./internal/gateway/; go vet
./...; GOOS=linux go vet -tags nogui ./...; GOOS=windows go vet ./...;
go build for darwin, linux, windows.

